<BaseLayout
  title="Why insurance prices differ"
  description="Why two insurers can quote very different prices for the same driver, car, or home."
>
  <a class="back-link" href="/learn/">&larr; All guides</a>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Why insurance prices differ between companies</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      It's common to get quotes for the same driver and car, or the same
      home, that are hundreds of dollars apart. That doesn't mean one insurer
      made a mistake — insurers price risk differently on purpose.
    </p>

    <p>
      Each insurance company builds its own underwriting model: its own view
      of how risky a given driver, vehicle, or property is, based on its own
      claims history, reinsurance costs, operating expenses, and the
      customers it's trying to attract. Two companies looking at the exact
      same application can reasonably reach different prices because they
      aren't using the same model.
    </p>

    <h2>Why some companies are cheaper</h2>
    <p>
      A lower price can come from several possible sources. None of these
      apply to every cheaper insurer — they're possible reasons, not a
      checklist that explains every low quote.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>A different underwriting or risk model that happens to favor your profile</li>
      <li>Lower operating costs, often from selling direct-to-consumer instead of through agents</li>
      <li>A customer base and claims experience that runs better than average for that insurer</li>
      <li>Telematics or usage-based programs that reward specific driving patterns</li>
      <li>Higher deductibles or narrower coverage built into the quote by default</li>
      <li>Promotional or new-customer pricing that may not hold at renewal</li>
      <li>A different appetite for the specific risk your application represents</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why some companies are more expensive</h2>
    <p>The same logic runs in the other direction:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Different underwriting assumptions or a higher estimate of your risk</li>
      <li>Higher claims or repair costs in that insurer's own experience</li>
      <li>Greater exposure to catastrophes (hurricanes, wildfire, hail) in your area</li>
      <li>Richer default coverage, lower default deductibles, or extra included endorsements</li>
      <li>A distribution model built around agents and service, which costs more to run</li>
      <li>Fewer discounts that happen to apply to your situation</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        <strong>"Cheaper" doesn't mean "better," and "more expensive" doesn't
        mean "more coverage."</strong> Before comparing price alone, check
        that you're comparing the same coverage, deductible, limits, and
        exclusions. Two quotes that look far apart may not be insuring the
        same thing.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      That's also why state law matters: insurers must follow the rating
      rules and required minimums set by each state's regulator, so what's
      allowed to affect your price — and what a policy must include — varies
      by where you live. See the verified rules for
      <a href="/auto-insurance/">auto insurance</a> and
      <a href="/home-insurance/">home insurance</a> by state.
    </p>

    <p>
      Want to check your own numbers against a state's requirements? Try the
      <a href="/tools/">Coverage Gap Checker</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
